# Linux User & Sudo Security Lab

## Overview

This hands-on cybersecurity lab demonstrates Linux user account management, authentication, authorization, sudo privileges, file permissions, and basic root account hardening.

The lab was performed in an Ubuntu Linux virtual machine using the Bash command line.

## Objectives

- Create and manage Linux user accounts
- Configure user passwords
- Verify account and group information
- Install and configure sudo
- Apply administrative privileges to a specific user
- Test access to protected system files
- Restrict interactive root login
- Verify user and group permissions
- Apply the principle of least privilege

## Lab Environment

| Category | Environment |
|---|---|
| Operating System | Ubuntu Linux |
| Virtualization | VMware |
| Shell | Bash |
| Security Tool | sudo |
| Network | NAT |

## Accounts Created

### Administrative User

`mlyons3`

This account was configured with sudo privileges for administrative tasks.

### Standard User

Mason NetID account

This account was intentionally not added to the sudo group to demonstrate restricted administrative access.

> The actual NetID is not published in this repository.

## Key Tasks

### 1. Create Linux User Accounts

Created the `omega3` administrative account:

```bash
useradd -c "Cocoa" -s /bin/bash -m omega3


2. Install Sudo

Updated the Ubuntu package repository and installed sudo:

apt update
apt install sudo
3. Configure Sudo Access

Added omega3 to the sudo group:

usermod -aG sudo omega3

Verified membership:

grep '^sudo:' /etc/group
4. Test Administrative Privileges

The administrative account was tested by attempting to access /etc/shadow.

A normal user should not be able to read the file directly:

cat /etc/shadow

The sudo-enabled user can request administrative access:

sudo cat /etc/shadow

Password hashes and other sensitive /etc/shadow contents are not included in this repository.

5. Restrict Root Login

The root account's login shell was changed to /usr/sbin/nologin:

sudo /usr/sbin/usermod -s /usr/sbin/nologin root

The configuration was verified:

grep '^root:' /etc/passwd

Expected configuration:

root:x:0:0:root:/root:/usr/sbin/nologin

An attempt to start a root login session using:

su - root

resulted in an authentication failure.

Security Concepts Demonstrated
Principle of Least Privilege

Only the administrative lab account was granted sudo privileges.

Authentication

User passwords were configured and account login behavior was tested.

Authorization

Different users were assigned different levels of access through group membership.

Privileged Access

The sudo command demonstrated controlled administrative privilege escalation.

File Permissions

Access to /etc/shadow was used to demonstrate restrictions on sensitive system files.

Account Hardening

Interactive root login was restricted using the nologin shell.

